Shape={{.prose_shape_roll}} | texture={{.sentence_texture_roll}} | focus={{.focus_roll}} | transition={{.transition_roll}} | restriction={{.anti_pattern_roll}}

{{if::{{.prose_shape_roll}} <= 2}}
Paragraph shape — negative space:
Use restraint. Let pauses, omissions, unfinished gestures, avoided eye contact, and delayed responses carry pressure. Do not resolve the emotional beat cleanly.

{{elseif::{{.prose_shape_roll}} <= 4}}
Paragraph shape — intimate close-focus:
Stay near one viewpoint's immediate perception. Use small specific observations rather than sweeping summary.

{{elseif::{{.prose_shape_roll}} <= 6}}
Paragraph shape — withheld interpretation:
Render action, posture, timing, and silence before naming what they mean. Let interpretation arrive late or remain partial.

{{elseif::{{.prose_shape_roll}} <= 8}}
Paragraph shape — action-threaded:
Let changes in position, distance, gesture, objects, or physical relation organize each paragraph. Interiority rides inside movement.

{{elseif::{{.prose_shape_roll}} <= 10}}
Paragraph shape — sensory anchor:
Choose one concrete sensory detail as the scene's spine and return to it once with altered meaning. Do not scatter unrelated sensory decoration.

{{elseif::{{.prose_shape_roll}} <= 12}}
Paragraph shape — dialogue-weighted drift:
Let dialogue carry more pressure, surrounded by interruptions, physical tells, and unspoken reactions. Do not explain every line afterward.

{{elseif::{{.prose_shape_roll}} <= 14}}
Paragraph shape — braided interiority:
Interweave external behavior and internal pressure. Thought should bleed into perception and syntax rather than forming repeated isolated monologue blocks.

{{elseif::{{.prose_shape_roll}} <= 16}}
Paragraph shape — snap-cut rhythm:
Use short-to-medium paragraphs with sharp cause-to-consequence turns. End one paragraph on gesture and begin the next with its effect.

{{elseif::{{.prose_shape_roll}} <= 18}}
Paragraph shape — slow-burn compression:
Use fewer, fuller paragraphs. Begin from external detail, imply internal reaction, and let action or dialogue interrupt before the feeling is named.

{{else}}
Paragraph shape — fracture pattern:
Use visibly uneven paragraph lengths. Begin one beat cleanly, let a longer paragraph overlap action and implication, and cut another before its emotional conclusion.
{{/if}}

{{if::{{.sentence_texture_roll}} <= 2}}
Sentence texture — blunt pressure:
Use plain, direct sentences when the beat sharpens. Simplicity should feel chosen rather than flat.

{{elseif::{{.sentence_texture_roll}} <= 4}}
Sentence texture — soft accumulation:
Let clauses build through observation and consequence while remaining concrete and readable.

{{elseif::{{.sentence_texture_roll}} <= 6}}
Sentence texture — interior interruption:
Allow one sentence to correct, interrupt, or sharpen itself. Do not turn every line into an em-dash construction.

{{elseif::{{.sentence_texture_roll}} <= 8}}
Sentence texture — delayed subject:
Occasionally begin with setting, object, gesture, or consequence before naming who acts. Use sparingly.

{{elseif::{{.sentence_texture_roll}} <= 10}}
Sentence texture — clean and tense:
Favor direct construction with deliberate length variation. Metaphor must reveal viewpoint rather than decorate.

{{else}}
Sentence texture — controlled variation:
Place one clipped sentence among longer ones and let one sentence turn midway through qualification, reversal, or delayed reveal.
{{/if}}

{{if::{{.focus_roll}} <= 2}}
Scene focus — environmental pressure:
Make room, weather, crowding, objects, distance, sound, or world rules alter what characters can do.

{{elseif::{{.focus_roll}} <= 4}}
Scene focus — contradiction:
Let body, words, and intent fail to align through restraint, pride, fear, affection, irritation, denial, or hunger.

{{elseif::{{.focus_roll}} <= 6}}
Scene focus — consequence:
Emphasize the material, social, or emotional result of the previous action rather than manufacturing a fresh twist.

{{elseif::{{.focus_roll}} <= 8}}
Scene focus — misread signal:
Let one visible action be read incompletely or incorrectly without making the observer foolish or breaking continuity.

{{else}}
Scene focus — power shift:
Center who gains, loses, refuses, or contests control through blocking, initiative, silence, leverage, or forced reaction.
{{/if}}

{{if::{{.transition_roll}} <= 2}}
Transition — silence gap:
Let one beat hang unanswered before the next action or line moves the scene.

{{elseif::{{.transition_roll}} <= 4}}
Transition — reaction first:
Begin at least one paragraph with the reaction before clarifying its immediate cause.

{{elseif::{{.transition_roll}} <= 6}}
Transition — hinge detail:
Use one repeated object, gesture, sound, or phrase as a bridge between paragraphs.

{{else}}
Transition — hard cut:
Permit a clean jump from cause to result when emotional and spatial logic remain legible.
{{/if}}

{{if::{{.anti_pattern_roll}} <= 2}}
Restriction:
Avoid stacked abstract nouns. Convert tension, fear, desire, guilt, and grief into behavior, choice, image, or consequence.

{{elseif::{{.anti_pattern_roll}} <= 4}}
Restriction:
